{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>These are the words that Moses spoke to all Israel.</b> Nowhere in all Scripture do we find that Moses spoke to all Israel as one — and is it even possible for him to speak with six hundred myriad men at a single time? And we do not find this expression in all Scripture except here and in the section of HaQhel, where it is stated (Deuteronomy 31:11): ״תִּקְרָא אֶת הַתּוֹרָה הַזֹּאת נֶגֶד כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּאָזְנֵיהֶם״, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 41a) that the king would read from ״אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים״ onward — for there is certainly a connection and resemblance between them, for throughout the Torah ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ is said, but here ״יִשְׂרָאֵל״ is said. Did he then speak to Israel? Was it not to the children of Israel that he spoke all his words?" "And it is nearly audible that in every place where he mentions ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, he is speaking to the totality of the masses, for they are all children of Israel; but in a place where he mentions ״יִשְׂרָאֵל״, he is speaking with the great ones of Israel, each of whom is called by the name Israel his grandfather and resembles him. And since upon the great ones of Israel is laid the obligation to rebuke the people, and if they do not rebuke them then the guilt of the people hangs upon them, therefore Moses urged them about this, for they have no room to exempt themselves from punishment by saying, 'It is manifest to us that rebuke is of no avail, for rebuke does not benefit a stiff-necked people' — for if before Him who is blessed it is manifest, before them who is manifest? And so the prophet held them culpable, saying (Jeremiah 2:8): ״וְאַחֲרֵי לֹא יוֹעִילוּ הָלָכוּ״ — they hung the absence of rebuke on the claim that it would not avail them, for they refused to hear; and this in truth is no claim, for in any case the one who rebukes must do what is his to do. And in any event he taught them the way of rebuke: for every rebuker who wants his words to be heard and not kicked back at, should rebuke them by hint and not explicitly, in such a way that no one will understand his words except this person who feels within himself that he is this man; but with an explicit rebuke he will surely kick back. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses that he should read this Torah — namely, the words of rebuke that are in this section — and they are ״נֶגֶד כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל״, for every rebuke consists of harsh words that stand opposed to all Israel; and he should read them ״בְּאָזְנֵיהֶם״ — that is, by hint, resembling one who speaks in a whisper, in such a way that it enters only into the ears of this one who knows himself that he is liable in the matter. And this is the hint in אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר מֹשֶׁה — to all the great ones who were called by the name Israel; and what did he speak to them? That was ״בַּמִּדְבָּר בָּעֲרָבָה״ etc. — meaning, from me you shall see and so shall you do, to rebuke by hint, for this is a great cause for the acceptance of rebuke." "And some say that it was precisely to the great ones — whom he called ״יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that he spoke by hint, on account of honor; but immediately afterward it is stated ״דִּבֶּר מֹשֶׁה אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that is, the masses of the people — and to them he allotted no honor, but explicitly told them the matter of the spies and the calf. And even though the great ones were presumably not implicated in all the matters that he spoke about by hint, nevertheless, since they did not rebuke them, he hung the blame upon them by hint. And it appears to me to resolve through this what the commentators asked: why was ״וַיְהִי בְּאַרְבָּעִים שָׁנָה״ not said at the beginning of the section. For the main reason for indicating this time is to make known that rebuke is best given close to death, so that one does not rebuke a person and then come back and rebuke him again — meaning, lest perhaps today or tomorrow he have room to say to the rebuker: 'Remove the beam,' etc. (Arakhin 16b); and as for such an evil thing, if it is possible to be found among the generality of the masses, nevertheless any man of valor, abundantly active, will not carry such speech upon his lips. Therefore the first rebuke, which was to the great ones alone, did not need this time to be written, for even at other times rebuke is good for them, for they would not brazenly say: 'Remove the beam' — for only a despicable wretch, gripped by cords of disgrace, would do so, and any man who fears Elohim would not let such speech be heard from his mouth. And furthermore, it is precisely those rebukes that were said explicitly, from ״וַיְהִי בְּאַרְבָּעִים שָׁנָה״ and onward, regarding which there is reason to be concerned lest one rebuke him and come back and rebuke him, so that he says: 'Remove the beam,' etc.; but this first rebuke, which was said by hint — there is no concern about this, for each person can pretend not to know." "Another matter: <b>the first rebuke, since Moses said it on his own initiative, therefore he mentioned it by hint,</b> for he feared lest he be burned by the coals, as happened to him when he said ״שִׁמְעוּ נָא הַמּוֹרִים״ (Numbers 20:10). But immediately afterward it is stated: ״דִּבֶּר מֹשֶׁה אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳ אוֹתוֹ אֲלֵיהֶם״ — he needed to say the matters as they were." "<b>Between Paran and between Tophel.</b> The expression ״בֵּין ... וּבֵין״ has no dawn or settlement at all according to Rashi's commentary. And furthermore it is difficult: why did he not hint, at least, at the matter of the waters of Meribah, as it is written (Psalms 106:32): ״וַיַּקְצִיפוּ עַל מֵי מְרִיבָה וַיֵּרַע לְמֹשֶׁה בַּעֲבוּרָם״ — if so, they were the ones who provoked HaShem, so why did Moses not mention it among all the provocations that they provoked? Rather, he surely hinted to them through the expression ״בֵּין וּבֵין״, for at Paran was the matter of the spies, and Tophel — its meaning is that they complained about the manna and said: ״וְנַפְשֵׁנוּ קָצָה בַּלֶּחֶם הַקְּלֹקֵל״ (Numbers 21:5). And in between — in between, the matter of the waters of Meribah occurred. And even though the dispute of Korah is written after the matter of the spies, nevertheless it preceded it, for behold Rashi explained ״וַחֲצֵרוֹת״ — this is the dispute of Korah, which took place in Hatzerot; and it is written (Numbers 12:16): ״וַיִּסְעוּ מֵחֲצֵרוֹת וַיַּחֲנוּ בְּמִדְבַּר פָּארָן״, and from there the spies were sent out. Hear from this that between the matter of the spies at Paran and the matter of Tophel, they had nothing with them except what they provoked at the waters of Meribah — as if to say: remember the deed that you did between these two boundaries. And this is a hint within a hint, because Moses too sinned in this, and he feared lest they say to him: 'Remove the beam,' etc.; therefore he buried this sin between what came before and what came after, for from them he drew proof that they were the cause, and the main guilt hangs upon you — for see what you did at Paran, where you said ״נִתְּנָה רֹאשׁ וְנָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה״ (Numbers 14:4), and likewise at Tophel you said ״לָמָה הֶעֱלִיתֻנוּ מִמִּצְרַיִם״ (Numbers 21:5), and likewise at the waters of Meribah you said ״לָמָה הֶעֱלִיתֻנוּ מִמִּצְרַיִם״ (Numbers 20:5). From this there is proof that every transgression drags along a transgression like itself, and what precedes and what follows indicates that even this sin that took place in between should be hung upon you and not upon me." "<b>Between Paran and between Tophel.</b> The language of 'between … and between' gives me room to set my hand a second time and to explain what lies between these two dates — between the seventeenth of Tammuz and the ninth of Av. For Paran is the matter of the spies, regarding which they wept a baseless weeping, and weeping was fixed for all generations on the night of the ninth of Av; and Tophel is the calf that they made. For what Rashi explained — that they slandered (תפלו) the manna — has no parallel in Scripture, and it would have been more fitting had Rashi explained it from the language of 'הֲיֵאָכֵל תָּפֵל מִבְּלִי מֶלַח' (Job 6:6), for any thing that has no taste is called תָּפֵל, and this would have accorded with Rashi's words. But according to our approach, it is closer to hear that the matter of the calf is alluded to in Tophel, for we find this term used for idolatry in several places: in Jeremiah (23:13) it is written: 'וּבִנְבִיאֵי שׁוֹמְרוֹן רָאִיתִי תִפְלָה הִנַּבְּאוּ בַבַּעַל' etc., and it is written (Lamentations 2:14): 'נְבִיאַיִךְ חָזוּ לָךְ שָׁוְא וְתָפֵל' etc., and likewise Ezekiel called them: 'טָחֵי תָפֵל' (13:11), and so too the makers of the calf said: 'אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל' (Exodus 32:4) — and with this they gave תִּפְלָה to Elohim. And this was on the seventeenth of Tammuz, and on that very day the tablets were broken; and at the breaching of these stones it was fixed for generations, for on the seventeenth of Tammuz the city wall was breached and its stones. And between these two boundaries Israel lies crouching between the straits, and this is the meaning of 'between … and between' stated here, as it is said (Lamentations 1:3): 'כָּל רוֹדְפֶיהָ הִשִּׂיגוּהָ בֵּין הַמְּצָרִים'. For between the sin of the calf and the sin of the spies they bent down, crouched, and could not rise from their rejection — for in the way of nature there is no salvation from the distress of an enemy except through one of two paths or through both as one: either from the side of the providence of HaShem, blessed be He, Who delivers all who cleave to Him even if they are not mighty in nature; whenever they fulfill 'וְאָהַבְתָּ אֶת ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ', which causes cleaving to HaShem, blessed be He, then He delivers all who cleave to Him; or from the side of nature, when they fulfill 'וְאָהַבְתָּ לְרֵעֲךָ כָּמוֹךָ' — then each man helps his fellow, even if they are sinners and HaShem has withdrawn from them; nevertheless they have some hope in the way of nature, if the alignment does not stand against them." "And in these two times Israel lost both as one. If so, to whom could they flee for help? For on the seventeenth of Tammuz, when they made the calf, on that very day they turned backward and turned to HaShem, blessed be He, the back and not the face — in the month whose sign is Cancer (סַרְטָן), whose way is to walk backward; thus they turned backward, and then they lost the salvation that comes from the direction of providence. And on the ninth of Av was the matter of the spies; on that very day the trait of baseless hatred was born in Israel, as it is said (Deuteronomy 1:27): 'וַתֵּרָגְנוּ בְאָהֳלֵיכֶם וַתֹּאמְרוּ בְּשִׂנְאַת ה׳ אֹתָנוּ הוֹצִיאָנוּ' etc. — Rashi explained: what is in your heart against your fellow, you say He has in His heart against you (Yalkut Shimoni, Deuteronomy תתה). For they reasoned: since each one of them hated his fellow, presumably the Holy One, blessed be He, hates them too. And furthermore, since they erred to say that the Holy One, blessed be He, hates them for nothing, then surely the nature of the matter demands this — that each one should hate his fellow. And this distress conceived and gave birth to this evil trait, which destroyed the First Temple and the Second. For in the First Temple there was baseless hatred among the princes of Israel, and in the Second Temple the affliction spread among all Israel, as is found in Yoma (9b). And this was in the month of Av, whose sign is Leo (אַרְיֵה), for each one, likened to a lion, lusted to tear his fellow apart — and then they lost also the natural salvation. For if there is no love among them and no one supports or helps his fellow, 'מִי יָקוּם יַעֲקֹב כִּי קָטֹן וְדַל הוּא' (Amos 7:2) — 'who could raise Jacob, for he is small and poor'? It is found, then, that between these boundaries the company was utterly disbanded: for they swiftly turned away from HaShem, and they also turned away, each man from his brother. And so it turns out that the seventeenth of Tammuz and the ninth of Av form a fence on this side and a fence on that side, and there is no path to swerve right or left. Therefore 'כָּל רֹדְפֶיהָ הִשִּׂיגוּהָ בֵּין הַמְּצָרִים' — 'כָּל' signifies that even a pursuer of any worth at all, were he weak, could prevail against them, for at one time they defied the Holy One of Israel, profaning the holiness of Israel that depends on the unity of the nation; and at the other time they turned backward from the Shekhinah — and to whom could they flee for help?" "Therefore Moses said in this rebuke: See that you stand between these two boundaries, between the straits — on the side of the matter of Paran and the matter of Tophel. And in order to spur them toward repentance he said thus, for if they would not return, then the matter of Paran would be fixed for them as weeping for all generations, for in it they rejected the Holy Land; therefore their sentence would be to be exiled from it — as in truth they were exiled on the ninth of Av. And through the matter of Tophel they would bring about the breaching of the city on the seventeenth of Tammuz. And between these two they would lie crouching between the straits, and an enemy of any worth at all would pursue them. And according to this explanation, we need to say that the word 'וְלָבָן' alludes to their quarrel regarding the manna, which was white (לָבָן), as Rashi explained; 'וְדִי זָהָב' alludes to the sin of the children of Gad and Reuben, whose wealth was dearer to them than their own selves — even though they had enough (דַּי)." "And in my work 'Ir Gibborim' I explained by way of allusion: 'אַחַד עָשָׂר יוֹם מֵחֹרֵב' — upon the eleven days that they mourn the destruction of the Temple during the year, namely the nine days of the month of Av, and the seventeenth of Tammuz, and the tenth of Tevet. And since this is far from the plain sense, I abbreviated it here. But the language of 'between … and between' bears this explanation even according to its plain sense."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {5 "The phrase עַל מַה דְּבֵינֵי בֵינֵי בֵין is Aramaic-tinged; rendered literally as 'what lies between these two bounds.'", 7 "The word-play דִּי זָהָב / דַּי ('enough gold' / 'enough') is preserved; the Kli Yakar reads the place-name as alluding both to gold and to sufficiency."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:05.837885568Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}